output-code-snippet2: |
    Prevent forged jshelter-bootstrap handshakes

    The Chrome/MV3 build passes the per-site configuration (including
    currentLevel, fpdWrappers, and the secret domainHash that seeds the
    Alea PRNG behind Canvas/WebGL/AudioContext farbling) to the
    MAIN-world wrapper script (wrappers_generated.js) through a
    CustomEvent channel on the shared page window.

    The ISOLATED-world content script (common/document_start.js) accepted
    a "jshelter-bootstrap" event from anyone, without sender validation.
    Because MAIN-world content scripts, ISOLATED-world content scripts,
    and page scripts share the same window as an EventTarget in Chromium,
    any script running in the page (including third-party iframes, as the
    content scripts match <all_urls> with all_frames: true) could:

    * dispatch its own bootstrap with a self-chosen portId,
    * send {init: true} over the forged channel, and
    * receive the full live configuration, including domainHash.

    Knowing domainHash lets the page reconstruct the Alea sequence and
    computationally undo the farbling noise, defeating the core
    anti-fingerprinting guarantee. It also discloses an extension-
    detection oracle (protection level and wrapped-API list) and allows
    forged fp-detection messages to be folded into the FPD database.
    Firefox/MV2 is not affected: wrappers are injected via
    nscl/content/patchWindow.js and XRay wrappers, no equivalent
    unauthenticated handshake exists.

    PoC (executes against an unpatched build after page load):

        window.__jshelter_leak = {};
        var portId = "attacker_controlled_port_id_1234";
        window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("jshelter-bootstrap",
            {detail: {portId: portId}}));
        window.addEventListener(portId + ":return:extension", function(e) {
            window.__jshelter_leak.config = e.detail && e.detail.value;
        }, true);
        window.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(portId + ":page",
            {detail: {msg: {init: true}}}));
        // __jshelter_leak.config now contains the config incl. domainHash

    Fix (common/document_start.js only, MAIN-world side unchanged):

    * The bootstrap listener is registered with {once: true} and guarded
      by a bootstrapSealed flag: only the first bootstrap binds a port.
      wrappers_generated.js runs at document_start in the MAIN world,
      i.e. before any page script can execute, so in a correctly working
      browser the first bootstrap is always the genuine one.
    * {init: true} is answered at most once (initAnswered): the pull
      path is one-shot, later pulls behave like "configuration not ready"
      and never disclose the config again.
    * e.detail.portId is validated before use; malformed bootstrap
      events no longer throw inside the listener.

    Alternatives considered:

    * Deliver the portId (or an HMAC secret) to both content scripts via
      a channel the page cannot write to, e.g. dynamic
      chrome.scripting world:"MAIN" injection. Rejected: reintroduces the
      race with page scripts that static manifest registration was meant
      to eliminate, and anything placed on window in the MAIN world is
      readable by the page anyway.
    * Apply the noise ISOLATED/background-side and hand the MAIN world
      only opaque already-noised values. Rejected: ISOLATED-world scripts
      cannot intercept page objects (prototypes belong to the page realm),
      so wrapping cannot happen there in Chromium.
    * Have the background service worker generate the portId and pass it
      in the configuration snapshot (as the pre-MV3
      chrome.userScripts-era code did). Rejected: the snapshot travels
      through NSCL mechanisms into the ISOLATED world, but the MAIN-world
      script still has to announce itself somehow, which leads back to an
      observable bootstrap.

    Known residual risks (documented in threatmodel.md):

    * A browser bug allowing page scripts to execute before
      document_start-injected code would defeat the ordering assumption;
      such a bug exposes unwrapped APIs to the page and is a problem
      beyond JShelter's scope.
    * Other installed extensions can inject their own code into the
      visited page, potentially earlier than JShelter. Such an extension
      can forge the first bootstrap, learn the per-session configuration
      (including the farbling seed), or prevent the wrappers from being
      applied at all. We cannot protect users against other extensions
      running in the page.

    Tests: unit tests in document_start_tests.js cover the sealed
    bootstrap, the one-shot init answer, malformed events, config push
    over an established port, and FPD forwarding; an integration test
    runs the PoC payload and fails if any configuration is disclosed.

